Explosive growth in AI model training demand is putting strain on datacenter cooling infrastructure capabilities. As GPU-intensive workloads proliferate, electricity consumption and cooling water demand surge simultaneously, and regional utility supply limits and environmental and water resource regulations have emerged as new axes of location competition. This goes beyond simple infrastructure cost increases to affect corporate regional strategies and long-term operational structures.

## AI Chip Demand and Infrastructure Bottleneck Mismatch

**NVDA** is at the center of this demand. As of November 12, 2026, closing price was $166.19, and despite a one-month decline of -24.90%, revenue growth maintained 105.9% year-over-year. The high net profit margin of 63.7% demonstrates the intensity of AI chip demand. However, recent stock price adjustments suggest that investors are increasingly viewing supply expansion constraints—particularly infrastructure bottlenecks—as a serious limiting factor.

## Rising Capital Intensity for Cloud Providers

**MSFT** is positioned to directly capture this demand through Azure. As of November 12, 2026, at $523.00 per share with a market cap of $3.89 trillion, it achieved 18.8% revenue growth. The net profit margin of 41.2% remains solid, but increased capital expenditure for datacenter cooling infrastructure expansion could threaten future profit margin maintenance. This is because high-power GPU cluster deployments are hitting the limits of existing cooling systems, shortening equipment replacement cycles.

## Dual Constraints of Power Supply and Freshwater Regulation

Increased power consumption in datacenters collides with regional grid supply capacity. As GPU density rises, cooling power increases exponentially, yet many regions lack new generation capacity. In negotiations with local utilities, cases of premium fees being imposed or expansion approvals themselves being delayed are increasing. Simultaneously, surging freshwater extraction for cooling is triggering community resistance and stricter environmental regulations. In water-stressed regions, new datacenter permits are being limited or mandatory cooling water recycling technology is being pursued, rapidly constraining corporate location choices.

## Structuring of Policy Risk

Multiple local governments are taking action to suspend or tighten conditions on new datacenter permits. Under pressure to achieve carbon neutrality targets, regulatory stance toward "high-power consumption industries" will be difficult to relax over time. This causes companies to lose strategic flexibility in location selection and increases dependence on secured capacity.

## Restructuring of Profit Structure

NVDA's high net profit margin of 63.7% means it has pricing power despite supply constraints. Large operators like MSFT possess the capital and government influence needed for new location development. Conversely, for smaller cloud providers or new datacenter operators, securing locations can become a matter of survival.

**CRM** is not a direct operator, but as of November 12, 2026, at $217.57 with 10.8% revenue growth, it can be indirectly affected by rising datacenter expansion costs among its customer base. As customers bear higher cloud costs, their spending decisions on software services like CRM may become more cautious.

Going forward, the key to datacenter competition will lie in low-power design, freshwater conservation technology, and building community trust. Companies that overcome infrastructure bottlenecks quickly will secure competitive advantage in the AI era.

